# The base problems of the CSES families repeated_cses_grid_paths_*: small grids of free squares and traps, the answer the number
# of right and down paths from the top left to the bottom right square that avoid every trap, modulo 10^9 + 7. Rows
# include a trap at the start or at the end (zero paths), a blocked diagonal and a trap in the middle.
#
# THE ANSWER of each row is the problem's definition computed exhaustively by a
# brute-force script (2026-11-21), which enumerates every candidate the problem's
# statement describes. PROOF: the program of the problem's file follows the
# definition, and its header argues under Definition 14 that its one normal form
# is that answer; the data are finite and within the family's bottom rungs, which
# the reference reading answers. The inputs are the author's own, no CSES test
# data.
#
#   grid_paths_point(?Point, ?Grid, ?Result)
#
# Grid is a Scott list (corpora/library/prelude/list) of rows, each a Scott list of Scott booleans, true for a free
# square and false for a trap (λthen_branch else_branch. then_branch, λthen_branch else_branch. else_branch); Result is an integer from_cbor of an integer literal
# (corpora/library/integer/cbor), the count modulo 10^9 + 7 (every count here is below the modulus).
